WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.400
Three people open the same link.

2
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.700
They see three different pages.

3
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.800
None of these were designed. They were decided.

4
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.500
Today, every visitor gets the same page.

5
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.100
Everything, in the same order. What you need is somewhere down there.

6
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.900
web4 gives each visitor the page that fits their moment.

7
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:29.200
How? Jev, a small decision model by TypeSafe, looks at a few facts about the visit.

8
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.000
From Instagram, on a phone, at 8:30 pm, from abroad.

9
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.800
For every part of the page, it answers three questions: show it? How? And where?

10
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.200
In about a quarter of a second, for a fraction of a cent.

11
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.700
It only picks from what the business already has. It never writes a word.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.700
To steer it, just say who something is for.

13
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.800
Now a guest who is already staying stops seeing the booking offer.

14
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.300
We tested it against hand-written rules.

15
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:05.300
With those rules taken away, the model was right 98% of the time. The rules alone: 73%.

16
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.300
But without saying who things are for, it broke some business rules on 2 of 4 sites.

17
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.100
So it doesn't replace rules yet. Every number is public.

18
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.800
Web 4 is here.

19
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.100
web4kit is open source. Try the playground, or start a site with one command.
